    "Here are the search results for PyTorch AMD GPU acceleration on Windows (2025-2026):\n\n## AMD ROCm on Windows (Native Support - New in 2025!)\n\nAMD has released native PyTorch support on Windows and Linux for AMD Radeon 7000 Series and 9000 Series GPUs and limited Ryzen AI 300 and AI Max APUs.(no content)Windows and Linux users can now take advantage of ROCm-enabled PyTorch wheels for AI model development. It's a preview release, meaning performance and feature coverage will continue to improve.\n\n### Latest Releases:\n- AMD Software: PyTorch on Windows Edition 7.1.1 is updated with AMD ROCm 7.1.1, available as a driver for Windows 11. (November 2025)\n- AMD Software: PyTorch on Windows Preview Edition 25.20.01.14 Driver is available for Windows 11. (September 2025)\n\n### Known Issues:\n- Intermittent script failure due to out-of-memory error may be observed on Ryzen AI Max 300 series processors. Intermittent application crash or driver timeout may be observed while running inference workloads alongside other applications like games or web browsers.\n- 32 GB is the minimum system memory recommended for PyTorch, with 64 GB recommended for larger models.\n\n## DirectML Option\n\ntorch-directml provides a DirectML backend for hardware acceleration in PyTorch, currently available for Public Preview. PyTorch with DirectML enables training and inference of complex machine learning models on a wide range of DirectX 12-compatible hardware.(no content)DirectML is a high-performance, hardware-accelerated DirectX 12 library for machine learning that provides GPU acceleration across all DirectX 12-capable GPUs from vendors such as AMD, Intel, NVIDIA, and Qualcomm.\n\n**Important Note:** DirectML is now in maintenance mode.\n\n## Performance Comparison\n\nROCm provides significantly better performance, running 4x faster than DirectML in some tests. The difference between DirectML and ROCm on the same hardware is dramatic—one user reports their RX 5700 XT taking 2 minutes on DirectML versus 29 seconds on ROCm for identical generation.(no content)DirectML is a general-purpose API not optimized specifically for AI workloads. ROCm provides GPU compute capabilities specifically designed for machine learning, similar to how CUDA works for NVIDIA.\n\n## Unsupported GPUs & Custom Builds\n\nFor users wanting to use ROCm 7 on unsupported AMD setups like RX 6800 and Radeon 780M, custom builds are possible. One developer documented building ROCm 7 + PyTorch with support for both GPUs on Windows.(no content)On Windows, hot-plugging works fine, but there's no official ROCm for some integrated GPUs, HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION doesn't work in Windows, and for older cards like RX 6800, the latest available official ROCm is 6.4.2. Vulkan acceleration works fine, but the ROCm driver may crash on Linux without proper support.\n\n## Recommendations for 2025\n\nComfyUI with ROCm is now the recommended approach for AMD users in 2025.(no content)PyTorch for AMD on Windows and Linux is now available as a public preview, enabling seamless AI workload execution on AMD hardware in Windows without any need for workarounds or dual-boot configurations."
